@endnode

@node Intro "Introduction"
@{b}Introduction@{ub}

webdav-handler is a filesystem handler for AmigaOS 4. It connects to
WebDAV servers over the internet (or your local network) and shows your
files on an ordinary volume, @{b}WebDAV:@{ub} (the device @{b}WDV:@{ub}).
WebDAV is offered by Nextcloud, ownCloud, many NAS boxes, hosting
services and web servers such as Apache.

Every server you set up with the prefs program @{b}wdvprefs@{ub} is a
drawer in the volume:

  WebDAV:
     Home/         your own Nextcloud
     Work/         the Nextcloud at work
     NAS/          the WebDAV share of a NAS

Opening a drawer connects to that server; the volume itself does not
connect anywhere, so it can be mounted at every start.

You can open the drawers on Workbench, copy files to and from them, create
drawers, rename and delete files, use them from the Shell and open files
from them in any program. Everything happens directly on the server: there
is no local copy and nothing to synchronise.

On a Nextcloud server the handler also keeps file dates and uploads big
files in parts, see @{"Server types" link Types}.

This handler was called Nextcloud Handler up to version 0.39, see
@{"From Nextcloud Handler" link Upgrade}.

@endnode

@node Requirements "Requirements"
@{b}Requirements@{ub}

You need the following. Everything that is not part of AmigaOS 4.1 Final
Edition must be installed before the handler can be used. The Install
script checks the versions and tells you if something is missing.

@{b}AmigaOS 4.1 Final Edition@{ub}
   Tested with AmigaOS 4.1 Final Edition Update 3.

@{b}filesysbox.library 54.8 or newer@{ub}
   Part of AmigaOS 4.1 Final Edition (Update 3 has version 54.9).

@{b}clib4.library 2.5 or newer@{ub}   (not part of AmigaOS)
   The C library the handler is built with.
   OS4Depot:  development/library/misc/clib4.lha
      https://os4depot.net/?function=showfile&file=development/library/misc/clib4.lha
   GitHub:    https://github.com/AmigaLabs/clib4/releases
   Install it with the Install script in the archive.

@{b}AmiSSL 5 (5.1 or newer) or AmiSSL 6@{ub}   (not part of AmigaOS)
   Used for the encrypted (https) connection to the server. Many users
   have it already; check that LIBS:amisslmaster.library exists and that
   "Version LIBS:amisslmaster.library" says 5.1 or newer.
   OS4Depot:  library/misc/amissl.lha
      https://os4depot.net/?function=showfile&file=library/misc/amissl.lha
   GitHub:    https://github.com/jens-maus/amissl/releases
   The handler uses AmiSSL 6 when it is installed, else the newest
   AmiSSL 5 there is. With DEBUG, the debug log names the AmiSSL used
   ("TLS: ..."); wdvprefs shows it under Test connection.

@{b}A working TCP/IP connection@{ub}
   Roadshow, as included in AmigaOS 4.1.

@{b}A WebDAV server@{ub}
   Its address, your user name and password. For Nextcloud use an app
   password, see @{"App password" link AppPassword}.

@endnode

@node Install "Installation"
@{b}Installation@{ub}

@{b}With the Install script (recommended)@{ub}

Double-click the @{b}Install@{ub} icon in the WebDAVHandler drawer. It
asks a few questions and then:

  - checks that the @{"required libraries" link Requirements} are installed,
  - offers to remove Nextcloud Handler, the earlier version, if it is
    installed (see @{"From Nextcloud Handler" link Upgrade}),
  - copies webdav-handler to L: and wdvprefs to SYS:Prefs,
  - copies the DOSDriver WDV to DEVS:DOSDrivers (mounted at every start)
    or SYS:Storage/DOSDrivers (mounted by hand),
  - copies the icon of the volume to ENVARC:Sys/def_WDV.info (and ENV:),
  - copies this guide to a place you choose,
  - mounts WDV: and starts wdvprefs, so that you can add your servers
    (see @{"Servers" link Servers}).

If WDV: is mounted already, the script stops it, replaces the handler and
mounts it again.

@{b}By hand@{ub}

1. Install clib4 and AmiSSL, see @{"Requirements" link Requirements}.
2. Copy L/webdav-handler to L:
3. Copy Prefs/wdvprefs (and its icon) to SYS:Prefs
4. Copy Devs/DOSDrivers/WDV (and its icon) to DEVS:DOSDrivers
5. Copy Prefs/Env-Archive/Sys/def_WDV.info to ENVARC:Sys and ENV:Sys
   (the icon Workbench shows for the volume)
6. Double-click the WDV icon, or type in a Shell:

      Mount DEVS:DOSDrivers/WDV

7. Start SYS:Prefs/wdvprefs and add your servers.

In DEVS:DOSDrivers, WDV is mounted at every start. The handler connects to
a server only when its drawer is first used, so it does not matter if the
network is not up yet when the system starts.

@endnode

@node Servers "Servers (wdvprefs)"
@{b}Servers (wdvprefs)@{ub}

The servers are set up with the prefs program @{b}SYS:Prefs/wdvprefs@{ub}.
Each server in its list is a drawer in WDV:.

  @{b}Add@{ub}, @{b}Duplicate@{ub}, @{b}Remove@{ub}
     Add a new server, copy the selected one, or remove it.

  @{b}Name@{ub}
     The name of the drawer in WDV:, for example "Home". Any name that
     is a valid AmigaDOS file name (no ":" or "/").

  @{b}Address@{ub}
     For Nextcloud the address you open in the browser, for example
     https://cloud.example.com or https://example.com/nextcloud (see
     @{"App password" link AppPassword}). For other servers the address of
     the WebDAV folder, for example https://nas.example.com/webdav.

  @{b}Type@{ub}
     Automatic (the default), Nextcloud or WebDAV, see
     @{"Server types" link Types}.

  @{b}User@{ub} and @{b}Password@{ub}
     Your user name and password (for Nextcloud an app password). Leave
     the password empty to be asked for it when the drawer is opened, see
     @{"Password" link Password}.

  @{b}Certificate@{ub}
     Only for a server with a certificate of its own: a PEM file with
     that certificate (or its CA certificate). Leave it empty for servers
     with a normal certificate. See @{"Security" link Security}.

  @{b}Do not check the server's certificate@{ub}
     Switches the check off. Unsafe, see @{"Security" link Security}.

  @{b}Test connection@{ub}
     Logs in with these settings and shows what it found (or why it
     failed): where the files are, the server type, the number of files
     and drawers and the free space. Nothing is saved.

@{b}Save@{ub} keeps the list in ENVARC:WebDAVHandler/servers.xml (and
ENV:), @{b}Use@{ub} only in ENV: until the next restart. The Edit menu has
"Reset to defaults" (an empty list), "Last saved" and "Restore".

The handler sees a changed list at once: new servers appear in WDV:,
removed ones go, and a changed server is connected again the next time its
drawer is used. Files that are open on a server keep working until they
are closed.

@endnode

@node Types "Server types"
@{b}Server types@{ub}

Any WebDAV server works. Nextcloud servers get two extras:

  - @{b}File dates@{ub}: SetDate and "Copy CLONE" keep the date of a file.
    Other servers set the date of the upload; setting a date there is
    accepted but has no effect (as with the protection bits).
  - @{b}Big files in parts@{ub}: files over 16 MB (CHUNKSIZE) are uploaded
    in parts, so server limits on the size of one request do not matter.
    If the server does not allow it, the file goes up in one piece.

On Nextcloud the handler also finds the WebDAV folder of your files from
the address you open in the browser.

The @{b}Type@{ub} in wdvprefs says how the handler treats a server:

  @{b}Automatic@{ub}  The handler asks the server at the address whether
             it is a Nextcloud server (one request when the drawer is
             first opened). If it is, it uses the extras and finds your
             files folder; if not, the address is the WebDAV folder to
             show. An address with /remote.php/ in it (the full WebDAV
             address of Nextcloud or ownCloud) is taken as Nextcloud.
  @{b}Nextcloud@{ub}  The server must be Nextcloud; the drawer reports an
             error if it is not.
  @{b}WebDAV@{ub}     The address is used as a plain WebDAV folder, without
             the extras, also on a Nextcloud server.

"Test connection" in wdvprefs shows the type found.

@{b}Other servers@{ub}

  - ownCloud: give the full WebDAV address, for example
    https://owncloud.example.com/remote.php/webdav. File dates are kept;
    uploads in parts are not used.
  - Apache (mod_dav), NAS boxes and hosting services: give the address of
    the WebDAV folder, with Type Automatic or WebDAV.

File comments are stored as WebDAV properties; most servers keep them. The
server must accept "Basic" login (https keeps the password safe on the
way).

@endnode

@node AppPassword "App password"
@{b}App password (Nextcloud)@{ub}

Nextcloud recommends a separate password for every device (an "app
password"). It is needed if your account uses two-factor authentication,
and it is better anyway: you can withdraw it at any time without changing
your real password.

In the Nextcloud web interface:

  1. Click your user picture (top right) and choose @{b}Settings@{ub}
     (or "Personal settings").
  2. Choose @{b}Security@{ub}.
  3. Under "Devices & sessions", enter a name such as "Amiga" and click
     @{b}Create new app password@{ub}.
  4. Nextcloud shows the user name and the password. Use exactly this user
     name (it may differ from the name you log in with) and this password.

@{b}Server address@{ub}: use the address you open in the browser, for example
@{b}https://cloud.example.com@{ub} or @{b}https://example.com/nextcloud@{ub}.
The handler finds the WebDAV address of your files by itself. The full
WebDAV address (https://.../remote.php/dav/files/USER) works as well; you
can see it in the Nextcloud Files app under "Files settings".

@endnode

@node Settings "Settings"
@{b}Settings@{ub}

The servers are set up with wdvprefs (see @{"Servers" link Servers}). The
@{b}Startup@{ub} line of the DOSDriver holds settings for the whole
volume; none of them are needed:

  Handler   = L:webdav-handler
  StackSize = 262144
  Priority  = 5
  GlobVec   = -1
  Activate  = 1
  Startup   = "VOLUME=Cloud CACHETIME=10"

Keywords:

  @{b}VOLUME=name@{ub}        The name of the volume (default: WebDAV).
  @{b}READONLY@{ub}           Do not allow any changes on the servers.
  @{b}CACHETIME=s@{ub}        How many seconds the handler trusts what it
                     read from a server before it asks again
                     (default 5). Changes made elsewhere show up after
                     this time.
  @{b}TEMPDIR=dir@{ub}        Where files being written are kept until they
                     are closed (default T:). Use a drawer on a hard disk
                     for very big files.
  @{b}KEEPDIR=dir@{ub}        Where a file is kept when it could not be
                     saved on the server (default: TEMPDIR).
  @{b}CHUNKSIZE=MB@{ub}       Files bigger than this are uploaded in parts
                     (default 16). Nextcloud only.
  @{b}TIMEOUT=s@{ub}          Give up when a server sends nothing for this
                     many seconds (default 60).
  @{b}CAPATH=dir@{ub}         Trust the certificates in this drawer
                     (default AmiSSL:Certs).
  @{b}PROXY=host:port@{ub}    Use an HTTP proxy.
  @{b}NOICONS@{ub}            Do not show drawer icons for the servers.
  @{b}CONFIGDIR=dir@{ub}      Read the server list from this drawer instead
                     of ENV:WebDAVHandler.
  @{b}CHARSET=name@{ub}       Character set of file names on the Amiga:
                     ISO-8859-1 or ISO-8859-15 (default: the system's,
                     ENV:Charset).
  @{b}DEBUG@{ub}              Write debug output, see @{"Troubleshooting" link Trouble}.

A value with spaces must be in quotes, and inside the Startup line those
quotes are written as *":

  Startup = "TEMPDIR=*"Work:Big Files*""

@endnode

@node Password "Password"
@{b}Password@{ub}

The password of a server is taken from the server list (see
@{"Servers" link Servers}). If it is empty there, the handler asks for it
the first time the server's drawer is used.

In the requester, tick @{b}Remember the password@{ub} to save it in the
server list (ENVARC:WebDAVHandler/servers.xml). It is saved only after
the server has accepted it.

If the server does not accept the password, the requester opens again. If
you click Cancel, the drawer shows "no disk" and the handler does not ask
again, so that Workbench does not keep opening the requester. To try
again, type in a Shell:

  DiskChange WDV:

To forget a saved password, clear it in wdvprefs and save.

@endnode

@node Using "Using the volume"
@{b}Using the volume@{ub}

@{b}The servers@{ub}

The top of the volume shows one drawer for each server in the list, with
a drawer icon. These drawers cannot be renamed, deleted or added to on
the volume itself; that is done with wdvprefs. Opening a drawer connects
to its server.

@{b}What works@{ub}

  - Reading and writing files of any size (also above 4 GB), copying,
    renaming, moving between drawers, deleting, creating drawers.
  - File dates on Nextcloud: "Copy CLONE" and SetDate keep the date on
    the server (see @{"Server types" link Types}).
  - File comments (FileNote) are stored on the server.
  - File names with national characters (a with ring, a and o with dots
    and so on) are converted between the Amiga character set and the
    UTF-8 names on the server. Characters the Amiga cannot show appear as
    %uXXXX; such files can still be opened, copied and renamed.
  - Upper and lower case: like on any Amiga volume, "readme.txt" finds
    "ReadMe.TXT". Writing a file with a name that differs only in case
    replaces the existing file.
  - Copying from one server to another, also between Nextcloud and other
    WebDAV servers.

@{b}How writing works@{ub}

A file you write is first stored in TEMPDIR (T: unless set otherwise) and
uploaded to the server when it is closed. Closing a big file therefore
takes as long as the upload. The volume is busy during that time.

If the upload fails (no space left on the server, connection lost, ...),
a requester tells you, and the data is kept in TEMPDIR (or KEEPDIR) so
that nothing is lost. The program that wrote the file is not told, as
AmigaOS cannot report errors when a file is closed.

@{b}Deleting drawers@{ub}

As on any Amiga volume, only empty drawers can be deleted. (The server
itself would delete a drawer with everything in it.) On Nextcloud, deleted
files go to the trash bin, where you can restore them in the web
interface.

@{b}Server not reachable@{ub}

When a server cannot be reached, a requester says why (once), and its
drawer reports "No disk in drive". The handler tries again at the next
access (at most every 10 seconds); nothing needs to be remounted.
"DiskChange WDV:" tries at once and shows the problem again.

@endnode

@node Upgrade "From Nextcloud Handler"
@{b}Upgrading from Nextcloud Handler@{ub}

This handler was called @{b}Nextcloud Handler@{ub} up to version 0.39
(L:nextcloud-handler, SYS:Prefs/nchprefs, device NCH:; up to 0.37 one
DOSDriver per server, NC0, NC1 and so on). WebDAV Handler replaces it.

@{b}The Install script@{ub} offers to remove Nextcloud Handler: it stops
NCH: and deletes L:nextcloud-handler, SYS:Prefs/nchprefs and the DOSDriver
NCH. A file that is still in use is renamed to .old and deleted by the
next installation.

@{b}wdvprefs@{ub}, the first time it starts (when there is no server list
yet), takes the servers over:

  - the server list of Nextcloud Handler 0.38 and 0.39
    (ENVARC:NextcloudHandler/servers.xml),
  - the DOSDrivers of version 0.37 and older in DEVS:DOSDrivers and
    SYS:Storage/DOSDrivers: the address, the user name, the password (from
    the Startup line or ENVARC:NextcloudHandler), and the CAFILE and
    INSECURE settings. The volume name becomes the name of the drawer.

After Save or Use, wdvprefs offers to delete the old settings: the old
DOSDrivers and ENV(ARC):NextcloudHandler.

An old DOSDriver (with URL= on its Startup line) that is mounted with the
new handler shows a requester that says so, and the device does not start.

@endnode

@node Security "Security"
@{b}Security@{ub}

@{b}Certificates@{ub}

The handler checks the server's certificate against the certificates
that come with AmiSSL (AmiSSL:Certs). Certificates from Let's Encrypt and
the other common authorities work without any setting.

A server with a self-signed certificate or your own certificate authority
(common on a NAS) fails the check. Do not switch the check off; instead
give the server's certificate (or the CA certificate) as a PEM file in
wdvprefs (@{b}Certificate@{ub}), for example ENVARC:WebDAVHandler/nas.pem.

"Do not check the server's certificate" switches the check off
completely. Then anyone between you and the server could read your files
and your password. Use it only for a quick test on your own network.

@{b}Plain http@{ub}

An http:// address sends the password and the files unencrypted. Use it
only on your own network.

@{b}Stored passwords@{ub}

The passwords in the server list are stored as plain text in
ENVARC:WebDAVHandler/servers.xml. For Nextcloud use app passwords (see
@{"App password" link AppPassword}), so that you can withdraw them at any
time.

@endnode

@node Limits "Limits"
@{b}Limits@{ub}

  - Protection bits (s, p, a, h and rwed) are not stored on the server.
    Setting them is accepted but has no effect. Every file shows as
    readable, writable, executable and deletable, so programs on the
    volume can be started. Scripts need "Execute", because the s bit is
    not kept.
  - File dates are kept on Nextcloud only; on other servers setting a
    date is accepted but has no effect.
  - Renaming that only changes upper and lower case ("hello" to "Hello")
    fails with "object already exists" with filesysbox.library 54.9.
    Rename it to another name first, then to the new one.
  - Names with characters the Amiga cannot show (other alphabets, emoji,
    and ":") appear as %uXXXX, for example "12%u003A30" for "12:30".
    They can be opened and renamed as usual.
  - Links are not supported.
  - A file or drawer cannot be moved from one server to another with
    Rename (the error is "packet request type unknown"): copy it, then
    delete the original.
  - Info on WDV: shows 1 TB free: the volume holds several servers, each
    with its own quota. wdvprefs' Test connection shows a server's free
    space.
  - Changes made on the server by other devices show up after CACHETIME
    seconds (and within a minute in a drawer you are writing to).
  - Files being written are kept in TEMPDIR until they are closed, so
    TEMPDIR needs room for the biggest file you write at once. With
    TEMPDIR on an FFS partition, files over 2 GiB can only be written
    from start to end (as Copy does), because FFS cannot seek past 2 GiB.
  - Only one program can write to a file at a time.
  - A file changed on the server during a read is noticed by its ETag.
    Some servers (Apache) give a changed file a "weak" ETag for one
    second; reading such a file within that second is not checked.
  - With filesysbox.library 54.9 (AmigaOS 4.1 FE Update 3) the handler
    converts file names itself, ISO-8859-1 or ISO-8859-15. Other character
    sets are not supported by this version.

@endnode

@node Trouble "Troubleshooting"
@{b}Troubleshooting@{ub}

@{b}The volume does not appear@{ub}
   Check that the libraries in @{"Requirements" link Requirements} are
   installed. The handler opens a requester when clib4, AmiSSL or
   filesysbox.library is missing or too old.

@{b}The volume shows no servers@{ub}
   The server list is empty or cannot be read: start wdvprefs and add
   your servers (see @{"Servers" link Servers}).

@{b}"No disk in drive"@{ub}
   The server of that drawer could not be reached or the login failed.
   The requester shown the first time says why. Check the server's
   settings with "Test connection" in wdvprefs. While a server cannot be
   reached, its drawer shows "no disk" and the handler tries again on the
   next access. A transfer that gets no data for TIMEOUT seconds (60 by
   default) also ends this way.

   After changing the server in wdvprefs, just open the drawer again;
   "DiskChange WDV:" also tries again at once and shows problems again.

@{b}"No Nextcloud server at ..."@{ub}
   The type of the server is Nextcloud, but the address does not lead to
   one. Check the address, or set the type to Automatic or WebDAV.

@{b}A requester about an old DOSDriver@{ub}
   A DOSDriver of Nextcloud Handler 0.37 or older is mounted with the new
   handler, see @{"From Nextcloud Handler" link Upgrade}.

@{b}"Object is in use" while reading a file@{ub}
   The file was changed on the server while it was being read. The read
   stops instead of mixing the old and the new version; copy the file
   again.

@{b}"Upload failed" requester@{ub}
   A file could not be saved on the server (network gone, server full,
   quota reached). Its data was kept in KEEPDIR (TEMPDIR if not set), as
   the requester says. Copy it to the volume again when the server works.

@{b}Certificate errors@{ub}
   See @{"Security" link Security}.

@{b}Debug output@{ub}
   Add DEBUG to the Startup line and mount the volume again. The handler
   then writes every request to the system debug log. To read it:

      DumpDebugBuffer RAM:debug.txt

   Look for the lines starting with [webdav-handler]. Please include
   them when you report a problem.

@endnode

@node History "History"
@{b}History@{ub}

@{b}0.42 (17.11.2026)@{ub}
   - Works with AmiSSL 6 alone and with every AmiSSL 5 from 5.1 on.
     Before, it needed AmiSSL 5.27 (older versions were refused, and
     AmiSSL 6 only helped when AmiSSL 5 was still installed).
   - wdvprefs' Test connection names the AmiSSL used.

@{b}0.41 (17.11.2026)@{ub}
   - Nextcloud Handler is now WebDAV Handler: webdav-handler, the device
     WDV:, the volume WebDAV: and the prefs program wdvprefs. Any WebDAV
     server works; Nextcloud servers keep their extras.
   - wdvprefs has a Type for every server: Automatic, Nextcloud or
     WebDAV, see @{"Server types" link Types}. Test connection shows the
     type found.
   - The Install script removes Nextcloud Handler on request, and
     wdvprefs takes its servers and passwords over, see
     @{"From Nextcloud Handler" link Upgrade}.
   - OS 4.1 style icons, and a volume icon for WDV: (def_WDV).
   - Setting a file date on a server that cannot keep it is accepted
     without effect, so that Copy CLONE works there.
   - Reading a file whose ETag is weak (Apache, for one second after a
     change) works; before, the read failed with "object is in use".
   - With a clib4.library older than 2.5 the handler shows a requester
     and the device does not start; before, the start (and with
     Activate = 1 the boot) waited for ever.

@{b}0.39 (17.11.2026)@{ub}   (Nextcloud Handler)
   - One device, NCH:, for all servers: every server is a drawer in the
     volume and is connected when its drawer is first used.
   - The new prefs program nchprefs keeps the list of servers, with a
     "Test connection" button. It takes the servers over from the
     DOSDrivers of earlier versions.
   - Remembered passwords go into the server list.
   - The character set of file names now comes from ENV:Charset when
     CHARSET is not given (it was always ISO-8859-1 before).
   - Long server messages are broken into lines in requesters.

@{b}0.37 (15.11.2026)@{ub}   (Nextcloud Handler)
   - Converting an empty text between the Amiga character set and UTF-8
     could give leftover memory instead of an empty text (no case is
     known where the handler showed it).

@{b}0.32 (15.11.2026)@{ub}   (Nextcloud Handler)
   - Reading stops with an error when the file is changed on the server
     during the read, instead of giving a mix of both versions.
   - The handler's code is freed when the volume is dismounted (about
     3 MB were lost with every mount before).
   - Problems that need the user, such as certificate errors, are shown
     in a requester once.
   - Names with ":" from the server can be used (shown as %u003A).
   - Files show the e bit, so programs on the volume can be started.
   - Line breaks in requesters are shown correctly.
   - Files over 2 GiB can be written with TEMPDIR on an FFS partition
     (FFS cannot seek past 2 GiB; the temp file is now used in order).

@{b}0.25 (15.11.2026)@{ub}   (Nextcloud Handler)
   First test version.

@endnode

